ltsp6-session-tuning
====================

What this package changes, and - more usefully - what it refuses to change.

The settings
------------

1. XDG_CACHE_HOME moves to /var/cache/ltsp6-sessions/<uid>

   ~/.cache is the busiest directory a desktop session owns: fontconfig
   caches, GTK icon caches, thumbnails, browser caches. With home
   directories on NFS every write goes over the network, once per session,
   and none of it is worth keeping between logins. Moving it to local disk
   is the single largest improvement in this package.

   The cache root is sticky and world-writable, like /tmp. The Xsession hook
   therefore tests "-O" - do we own the directory - before using it. If
   another user pre-created a directory for our uid, the test fails and the
   session quietly keeps the default ~/.cache. Degraded, never exploited.

   Consequence worth knowing: a user's cache does not follow them between
   application servers. That is correct for a cache, and it is why the load
   balancer's session affinity matters for feel rather than function.

2. xfwm4 compositing off and vblank_mode=off by default

   Without a GPU in the session, Xorg runs on llvmpipe and every shadow,
   fade and transparent panel is composited by the CPU, once per session.
   With a GPU (glamor), the cost moves to the render engine, and xfwm4's
   default vblank_mode "auto" waits for a vertical blank that xorgxrdp's
   virtual display does not deliver: the desktop then feels sluggish.
   vblank_mode=off avoids that even for users who turn compositing back on.

   These are defaults. A user who already has their own xfwm4 settings in
   ~/.config/xfce4/xfconf/xfce-perchannel-xml/xfwm4.xml keeps them; check
   existing homes for use_compositing=true if you migrate an old server.

3. inotify headroom and vfs_cache_pressure

   The inotify limits are per-uid, so they are not shared between sessions -
   but one Xfce session spends them freely (xfsettingsd, xfdesktop, Thunar,
   panel plugins, gvfs, tumbler, dconf, then the user's own applications).
   Exhaustion surfaces as EMFILE from inotify_init, usually printed as "too
   many open files", which sends people looking at file descriptor limits
   instead. Headroom costs a few kilobytes per instance.

   vm.vfs_cache_pressure = 50 halves how eagerly the kernel drops dentry and
   inode caches. With NFS homes each dropped entry becomes a network round
   trip on the next lookup, and desktops do a great many small lookups. If
   your home directories are local, the default of 100 is fine and this line
   can go.


4. memlock 64 MB per process

   /etc/security/limits.d/30-ltsp6-session-tuning.conf. systemd's default is
   8 MiB. Programs that keep secrets in locked memory so they never reach
   swap need more: OTPClient (libgcrypt secure memory) shows "memlock value
   too low" below its recommended 64 MB. xrdp's PAM stack runs pam_limits,
   so the value reaches every new RDP session; existing sessions keep the
   old limit until the next login.

   It is a per-process ceiling, not a reservation, and locked pages still
   count against the user's memory cgroup.

5. Browser disk cache 256 MB

   /etc/firefox-esr/ltsp6-session-tuning.js and
   /etc/thunderbird/pref/ltsp6-session-tuning.js (both directories are read
   through Debian's defaults/syspref link). With XDG_CACHE_HOME on local
   disk the browsers' disk caches land in /var/cache/ltsp6-sessions too, and
   Firefox would size each one up to 1 GB. Plan the space anyway: with many
   users, give /var/cache/ltsp6-sessions its own filesystem rather than
   sharing /var with logs and apt. The 30-day tmpfiles age only removes the
   caches of users who stopped logging in.

6. Dirty page cache in absolute numbers

   vm.dirty_background_bytes = 256 MB and vm.dirty_bytes = 1 GB replace the
   percentage defaults, which on a host with 192 GB allow ~38 GB of unwritten
   data before forcing it out in one go. That shows up as kworker "flush"
   threads and sessions freezing while they write.

7. Desktop search indexer off

   localsearch (tracker) indexes every home directory: on a session server
   that is I/O and CPU per user for a search few use there. The autostart
   entries are shadowed by Hidden=true copies in /etc/xdg/ltsp6/autostart,
   and localsearch-3.service only starts if /etc/ltsp6/enable-desktop-search
   exists.

8. Screen saver: blank, locking, 5 minutes

   An animated screen saver in an idle RDP session is drawn, encoded and
   sent for nobody to see. /etc/default/ltsp6-session-tuning sets mode, lock
   and timeout; the Xsession hook writes them into ~/.xscreensaver at every
   login (creating it if missing) and starts xscreensaver even if the user
   hid its autostart entry. SCREENSAVER_POLICY=no leaves all of it alone.
   Removing xscreensaver-gl takes the OpenGL hacks away entirely; do not
   remove xscreensaver-data, xscreensaver depends on it.


Not in this package, but worth setting locally
-----------------------------------------------

  * Per-user limits in a user-.slice drop-in, for example
    /etc/systemd/system/user-.slice.d/50-local.conf:
        [Slice]
        CPUQuota=250%
        MemoryHigh=4G
        MemoryMax=5G
        MemorySwapMax=4G
    Without them one session can take the host down; with values that are
    too tight a session freezes while the kernel throttles it and drops its
    program code (look at "high" in memory.events and workingset_refault_file
    in memory.stat of user-<uid>.slice). The numbers depend on RAM, swap and
    how many users run IDEs or several browsers - measure, do not copy.

  * On hosts with Intel GPUs in a VM: keep the cards out of runtime PM
    (udev rule setting power/control=on for xe and i915). The multi-GPU
    setup of xrdp-vaapi does that; without it a card can suspend under a
    running session and freeze it.


What was deliberately left out
------------------------------

Each of these appears in most "multi-user tuning" advice. They were measured
on Debian 13 before being rejected, so nobody has to spend an afternoon
re-adding them.

  * UserTasksMax / DefaultTasksMax
    systemd ships /usr/lib/systemd/system/user-.slice.d/10-defaults.conf with
    TasksMax=33%, and Debian sets kernel.pid_max = 4194304 in
    /usr/lib/sysctl.d. That is roughly 1.38 million tasks per user. It is not
    the limit anyone is hitting.

  * kernel.pid_max
    Already 4194304 from Debian's own sysctl.d. Raising it again changes
    nothing.

  * vm.max_map_count
    Already 1048576 from Debian's own sysctl.d.

  * nofile limits
    systemd's default soft limit is generous for a desktop session and the
    hard limit is 1048576. Raising the soft limit globally mostly helps
    programs that allocate by the limit rather than by need.

  * vm.swappiness
    A genuine trade-off in both directions, not an improvement. Lowering it
    keeps idle sessions resident, which helps a returning user - but it also
    makes the kernel drop page cache instead, and with NFS homes that cache
    is expensive to refill. Left at the default on purpose; tune it against
    your own memory pressure, not against advice.


Thumbnails on network home directories
--------------------------------------

Moving the cache off NFS stops tumbler *writing* thumbnails over the network,
but it still *reads* the files to make them. On a host with many sessions and
large media in home directories that read load can be worth stopping.

This package does not ship it, because /etc/xdg/tumbler/tumbler.rc is a
conffile belonging to tumbler-common and a package must not fight another
package for a file. Apply it locally instead - the key names below are from
that shipped file:

    # /etc/xdg/tumbler/tumbler.rc
    [FfmpegThumbnailer]
    Excludes=/home
    [GstThumbnailer]
    Excludes=/home
    [RawThumbnailer]
    Excludes=/home
    [PopplerThumbnailer]
    MaxFileSize=8388608

"Excludes" takes a ;-separated path list and, per tumbler's own
documentation, takes precedence over "Locations". The other sections are
JPEGThumbnailer, PixbufThumbnailer, CoverThumbnailer, FontThumbnailer,
OdfThumbnailer, EpubThumbnailer and DesktopThumbnailer.


Checking that it took effect
----------------------------

    # in a session, after logging in again:
    echo "$XDG_CACHE_HOME"            # -> /var/cache/ltsp6-sessions/<uid>
    xfconf-query -c xfwm4 -p /general/use_compositing   # -> false
    sysctl fs.inotify.max_user_instances vm.vfs_cache_pressure
    ulimit -l                         # -> 65536
    xfconf-query -c xfwm4 -p /general/vblank_mode       # -> off
    grep -E '^(mode|lock|timeout):' ~/.xscreensaver

The Xsession hook only runs for sessions started after installation. Existing
sessions keep the old environment until the user logs out and back in.
